Kubernetes objects…

Kubernetes is built on objects, Mastering them makes the difference between “just running containers” and running production-ready apps.

Here are 20 important Kubernetes Objects/Components that you should definitely know:

🔹 Core Building Blocks

Namespace – Provides logical isolation and organization within a cluster.
Pod – The smallest unit in Kubernetes, usually running one container (or tightly coupled ones).
Service – Exposes Pods with a stable IP/DNS name and can load balance traffic.
ConfigMap – Stores non-sensitive configuration data in key-value pairs.
Secret – Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, and certificates in encoded form.

🔹 Workload Management

ReplicaSet – Ensures a specified number of Pod replicas are always running.
Deployment – Declaratively manages ReplicaSets for rolling updates and scaling.
StatefulSet – For applications that need stable network identity and persistent storage (e.g., databases).
DaemonSet – Ensures a Pod runs on every (or selected) node, often for logging/monitoring.
Job – Runs Pods until a task finishes successfully (batch workloads).
CronJob – Schedules Jobs at fixed intervals, like backups or reporting tasks.

🔹 Networking & Access

Ingress – Manages external access to Services (HTTP/HTTPS) with routing rules & TLS.
NetworkPolicy – Defines how Pods communicate with each other and external services, improving security.

🔹 Security & Governance

Role & RoleBinding / ClusterRole & ClusterRoleBinding – Provide fine-grained access control with RBAC.
ResourceQuota – Sets overall resource limits (CPU, memory, storage) per Namespace.
LimitRange – Enforces minimum & maximum resource requests/limits for Pods or Containers.

🔹 Storage

PersistentVolume (PV) – Cluster-level storage resource provisioned manually or dynamically.
PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC) – A Pod’s request for storage that binds to a PV.
StorageClass – Defines storage types & enables dynamic provisioning of volumes.
🔹 Autoscaling

HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA) – Automatically scales Pods based on CPU, memory, or custom metrics.

✅ This order reflects how most people learn & use Kubernetes — starting from Namespaces & Pods, moving through Deployments/StatefulSets, then Networking & Security, and finally Storage & Autoscaling.
